 "title": {
  "t": "Implications for anonymity",
  "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
 },
 "takeaway": {
  "t": "Defenses are limited",
  "src": "paper/sections/02_introduction.tex"
 },
 "steps": [
  {
   "view": "known",
   "t": "Once a model is familiar with a writer, it is hard to stay anonymous.",
   "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex",
   "more": {
    "t": "Even when a text is brand new, a model can identify its writer from what it learned about them elsewhere; i.e., once that knowledge is in the weights, the writer cannot do anything about it.",
    "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
   }
  },
  {
   "view": "refusals",
   "t": "Refusals are rare.",
   "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex",
   "more": {
    "t": "Fable 5.2 and Opus 5.5 refuse most often, on under 2% of all documents and mostly on public peer reviews.",
    "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
   },
   "details": [
    {
     "t": "Refusals are far rarer through the Anthropic API than through Claude Code.",
     "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
    },
    {
     "t": "Claude Fable 5.2 returns a written refusal on 323 of 1,258 Reddit posts through Claude Code but only 3 (0.2%) through the batch API, which our results use.",
     "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
    },
    {
     "t": "We additionally identify 162 written abstentions from Fable 5.2 and 130 from Opus 5.5, mostly citing insufficient evidence; only 50 of Fable 5.2's abstentions mention privacy or concerns about incorrect attributions.",
     "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
    },
    {
     "t": "With our observed low refusal rates and rapidly improving models, another security leak is not needed to undermine the blind peer reviewing process; it could take just a few prompts.",
     "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "view": "para_setup",
   "t": "We test paraphrasing as a defense against author identification, using Argon at medium effort to revise texts.",
   "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex",
   "more": {
    "t": "The prompt asks it to make the author's style less identifiable while preserving content and meaning, a form of authorship obfuscation.",
    "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
   }
  },
  {
   "view": "para",
   "t": "Paraphrasing lowers accuracy, though more familiar authors remain easier to identify.",
   "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex",
   "more": {
    "t": "On Reddit, Opus 5.5 drops from 16.8% to 10.4%.",
    "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
   }
  },
  {
   "view": "stories",
   "t": "Of the 34 stories whose authors Opus 5.5 rates very familiar (2.5–3 on the 0–3 scale), it names 32 authors before rewriting and 27.5 after, on average over the two rewrites.",
   "src": "paper/sections/06_discussion.tex"
  },
  {
   "view": "defenses",
   "t": "Defenses are limited:",
   "src": "paper/sections/02_introduction.tex",
   "items": [
    {
     "icon": "chat-x",
     "t": "models rarely refuse these queries",
     "src": "paper/sections/02_introduction.tex"
    },
    {
     "icon": "eye-off",
     "t": "deleting one's online writing does not remove it from the weights of existing models",
     "src": "paper/sections/02_introduction.tex"
    },
    {
     "icon": "rewrite",
     "t": "rewriting human-authored text with AI only helps partially",
     "src": "paper/sections/02_introduction.tex"
    }
   ]
  }
